**Read:** [Measurement uncertainty arising from sampling — Ramsey, Ellison, and Rostron](../../../../hephaestus/inquiry/metrology/sources/Sampling-Uncertainty.lingua#r_3Q0NEPSBDFX8Z1JWAG3C3HRHJM), the whole conceptual treatment and the relevant complete design examples; [Levantamento da geodiversidade — SGB technical guide, volume 5 version 4](../sources/SGB-Geodiversity-Methods.lingua#r_7QW5KYAYK9SB2MT3R6MNCFAQWK), the sampling/observation and interpretation method; [Laboratory Manual for Introductory Geology — Deline, Harris, and Tefend](../sources/Introductory-Geology-Laboratory-Manual.lingua#r_7G68RZ432EQWTB84DSVJ4JCZ2Z), the relevant specimen/identification preparation.

A sampling design depends on what the result is about: one specimen, a lithological unit, a deposit, a stratigraphic interval, a water body, or a spatial/temporal population. Define that target, boundaries, heterogeneity, accessible observations, and preparation/analysis chain before choosing a convenient set of specimens.

Spatial clustering, exposure bias, selective collection, weathering, grain-size sorting, and preparation can change an inferred distribution or composition. Several analyses of one split specimen assess different information from observations across a heterogeneous body. A visually striking sample may be useful for identification while unsuitable for an unbiased bulk-composition claim.

Preserve the geological context as well as statistical design. A regular grid that mixes distinct units can obscure the process being investigated; a hypothesis-targeted traverse may answer a structural question without supporting an unbiased regional mean. State the inferential scope rather than treating one preferred design as universal.

**Work:** design one hypothetical mapping/identification study and one composition/population study in a defined geological setting. Explain their different sampling choices, access limitations, possible bias, replication, and independent checks. Trace one result from target through field collection and laboratory preparation to interpretation.

Read the whole sampling guide's conceptual and relevant empirical/model sections alongside the regional-method reference. Quantitative uncertainty, geological boundaries, and chemical speciation need a connected account. The subsequent material sequence develops the physical/chemical knowledge needed to recognize what has actually been sampled.

